  "title": "Inequalities",
  "content": "<p><strong>Inequalities</strong> use \\(<, >, \\leq, \\geq\\) instead of \\(=\\). Solve them like equations, with one key rule: <strong>when you multiply or divide by a negative number, reverse the inequality sign</strong>.</p><p><strong>Number line representation:</strong> Open circle ○ for \\(< / >\\) (not included), filled circle ● for \\(\\leq / \\geq\\) (included).</p><p><strong>Double inequalities:</strong> \\(a < x \\leq b\\) means \\(x\\) is between \\(a\\) (not included) and \\(b\\) (included). To solve, do the same operation to all three parts.</p><p><strong>Integer solutions:</strong> List whole numbers that satisfy the inequality.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(3x + 2 > 14\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Subtract 2: \\(3x > 12\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Divide by 3: \\(x > 4\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(x > 4\\)</p>"
 },
